ore; short catalyst residence followed by cooling and aldehyde trapping.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Partial nitrile hydrogenation gives an aldimine, whose hydrolysis releases ammonia and the aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and potentially prohibitive. Nitrile-to-aldehyde chemistry requires stopping reduction at the imine and favoring hydrolysis over further hydrogenation; no suitable native-mineral selectivity is established here. The aminoaldehyde also strongly favors cyclic imine formation. This edge must not be treated as a demonstrated hydrothermal source of the Strecker substrate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidination before aminoaldehyde formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated, partially evaporated solution at pH approximately 9-10.5; residual water maintained; UV-shielded pore or sediment layer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal primary amine adds to the cyanamide nitrile carbon, followed by proton transfers to form a substituted guanidine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanamide-mediated amine guanidination is chemically established, but this substrate-specific prebiotic application is speculative. Performing it before alpha-amino-group installation avoids competition between two primary amines. Competing aminonitrile cyclization and cyanamide dimerization may prevent useful conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Partial reduction of the guanidinated C4 nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Nickel-bearing metal surface, hypothetical partial-reduction catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"50-150 bar total pressure; limited hydrogen activity\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Nickel-bearing surface; required chemoselectivity unvalidated\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly acidic, water-rich microenvironment; rapid product desorption and cooling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal nitrile is partially hydrogenated to an imine and hydrolyzed to an aldehyde, with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. This alternative attempts to reduce aminoaldehyde cyclization by installing the less nucleophilic guanidino group first. It still lacks a demonstrated prebiotic catalyst that stops at the aldehyde, and guanidine adsorption could poison the metal or alter selectivity. Preservation of the guanidino group is an additional requirement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidination of 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanamide; pH approximately 8.5-10; freshly supplied aminoaldehyde; sheltered from direct UV.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The open-chain terminal amine adds to cyanamide to generate a guanidino substituent while retaining the aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate application of established guanidination chemistry. Early guanidination avoids alpha-versus-side-chain amine competition later, but open-chain 4-aminobutanal may be only a minor species because of rapid five-membered cyclic imine formation. Aldehyde condensation and cyanamide side reactions are unresolved competitors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker extension to ornithinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia- and cyanide-containing pool, pH approximately 8-9.5; continuous aminoaldehyde supply; attenuated UV in sediment pores.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine with the aldehyde; cyanide addition installs the new carbon and alpha-amino functionality.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker chemistry is well established for suitable aldehydes, but efficient reaction of this unstable aminoaldehyde under the stated conditions is speculative. Intramolecular imine formation can outcompete intermolecular ammonia capture, and the product can undergo additional cyclization. No enantioselectivity is supplied.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker extension of 4-guanidinobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich, cyanide-containing water at pH approximately 8-9.5; guanidino group predominantly protonated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde-imine formation followed by cyanide addition produces the alpha-aminonitrile carrying a preformed guanidino side chain.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically motivated but substrate-specific speculative Strecker reaction. Guanidinium protonation should reduce side-chain nucleophilicity relative to a free primary amine, potentially reducing cyclization. Actual aldehyde lifetime, competing condensation and aminonitrile yield remain unestablished.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-chain guanidination of ornithinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanamide-containing solution; pH approximately 9-10.5; limited reaction time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal side-chain amine adds to cyanamide, generating the guanidino group without changing the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative with respect to regioselectivity. Both the alpha-amino and terminal amino groups can react; their different electronic environments do not establish exclusive side-chain guanidination. The graph represents the target-forming product channel, not a predicted selective synthesis. Intramolecular nitrile capture and multiple guanidination are competing reactions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of ornithinonitrile to ornithinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic water, approximately pH 1-3; moderate residence time followed by cooling if amide accumulation is desired.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted water addition to the nitrile and proton transfers generate the primary carboxamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydration is established chemistry, and protonation suppresses some amine-driven cyclization. Accumulating the amide rather than proceeding directly to the acid is a kinetic requirement, not an established environmental outcome. Product-specific rates and survival in acidic hydrothermal water require measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-chain guanidination of ornithinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cooling and pH adjustment after nitrile hydration; pH approximately 9-10.5; partial evaporative concentration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amino group adds to cyanamide to form argininamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific application of cyanamide guanidination. Converting the nitrile to an amide changes electrophilicity and cyclization opportunities, making this a distinct chemical ordering. It does not protect the alpha-amino group, so wrong-site and double guanidination remain serious limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of ornithinamide to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-410 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-150 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic pore water, approximately pH 1-3; prolonged residence followed by cooling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed carboxamide hydrolysis produces the amino acid and ammonia, predominantly present as ammonium at the reaction pH.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide-to-acid hydrolysis is chemically well founded and completes the conventional Strecker sequence. The proposed setting supplies heat and acidity without oxygen or enzymes. Whether hydrolysis outpaces competing degradation at naturally attainable acid concentrations is uncertain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Terminal guanidination of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated solution at pH approximately 9-10.5; residual water during evaporation; protection from direct UV.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Addition of the ornithine side-chain amino group to cyanamide gives the arginine guanidino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanamide is a chemically credible guanyl donor, but selective conversion of unprotected ornithine under these geochemical conditions is not established here. Alpha-amino guanidination, multiple guanidination and cyanamide self-reaction compete. This is the shortest terminal assembly step, not a claim that arginine will be the dominant product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of argininonitrile to argininamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic water at approximately pH 1-3; guanidino group protonated; controlled thermal exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted nitrile hydration forms the primary amide while retaining the guanidino substituent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The functional-group transformation is established in general. Its useful rate and chemoselectivity on this substrate in a natural fluid remain speculative. Acidic conditions reduce nucleophilic side-chain reactions, but they do not guarantee quantitative guanidino-group survival.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of argininamide to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-410 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-150 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic aqueous compartment, approximately pH 1-3; cooling after hydrolysis to limit further decomposition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carboxamide hydrolysis releases ammonia and produces the arginine carboxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This uses ordinary amide hydrolysis rather than biological amidases. Arginine can survive some acidic hydrolysis conditions, but the balance between the required reaction time and substrate degradation must be established for the proposed fluid. No prebiotic yield is asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs capture of ornithinonitrile as a hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonium-carbonate-containing water at pH approximately 8-10; sufficient dissolved inorganic carbon; shaded concentrated pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbon dioxide capture by the alpha-amino group and subsequent cyclization and rearrangement give the hydantoin; this node represents the net Bucherer-Bergs conversion.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin formation through alpha-aminonitrile and inorganic-carbon chemistry is an established alternative to direct Strecker hydrolysis. Applying it selectively to this diamino substrate is speculative because the terminal amine also reacts and can promote cyclization. The hydantoin branch is included because it masks the alpha-amino group during later guanidination.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidination of ornithine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanamide; mildly alkaline water, approximately pH 8.5-10; avoid extended strong-base exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The exocyclic primary amine adds to cyanamide, retaining the hydantoin ring as an alpha-amino-group mask.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative but chemically motivated: incorporation of the alpha nitrogen into a carbonyl-containing ring should reduce its competition with the terminal primary amine. Deprotonated hydantoin nitrogens may nevertheless react, and cyanamide self-condensation competes. Improved regioselectivity must be demonstrated rather than assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-opening hydrolysis of ornithine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"50-200 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Base-promoted hydrolysis by hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline water, approximately pH 10-11; prolonged residence may be necessary; subsequent cooling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the hydantoin ring produces the N-alpha-carbamoyl amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin ring opening is established chemistry, but laboratory procedures can require harsher conditions than a natural alkaline vent supplies. Therefore useful conversion in this temperature and pH window is speculative. Accumulation of the open-chain carbamoyl compound creates a distinct site-protected guanidination opportunity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidination of N-alpha-carbamoylornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cooled, concentrated solution at pH approximately 9-10.5; alpha-carbamoyl group retained during cyanamide exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The free side-chain primary amine adds to cyanamide while the alpha nitrogen remains carbamoylated.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate application with a rational selectivity advantage: the carbamoylated alpha nitrogen is less nucleophilic than a free alpha amine. This avoids requiring cyanamide to discriminate between two unprotected primary amines. Carbamoyl stability and target-product selectivity under repeated concentration cycles remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Decarbamoylation to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"380-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-150 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Transfer from alkaline water to an acidic pore, approximately pH 1-3; sufficient residence for carbamoyl cleavage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group liberates ornithine; decomposition of the released carbamic-acid equivalent yields carbon dioxide and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nonenzymatic removal of an N-carbamoyl group is chemically possible but often demanding. The proposed environmental rate is uncertain and may be too low before other degradation occurs. This completes the hydantoin route to ornithine without introducing a biological carbamoylase.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs capture of argininonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonium-carbonate-containing solution at pH approximately 8-10; guanidino side chain mostly protonated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbon dioxide incorporation and cyclization of the alpha-aminonitrile generate the arginine-substituted hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a speculative substrate extension of established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. A protonated guanidino group should compete less strongly than a free terminal amine, but neither hydantoin yield nor side-chain survival is established under the proposed prebiotic conditions. The incorporated carbon is subsequently released during decarbamoylation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-opening hydrolysis of arginine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"50-200 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Base-promoted hydrolysis by hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline water at approximately pH 10-11; thermal exposure minimized after ring opening.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis opens the hydantoin ring to N-alpha-carbamoylarginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The ring-opening reaction class is established, but this substrate and geochemical operating window are speculative. A major selectivity problem is achieving hydantoin hydrolysis before guanidino-group hydrolysis or other decomposition. The graph does not assume that prolonged alkaline heating preserves arginine functionality.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_024\",\n      \"name\": \"Decarbamoylation to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"380-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-150 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic aqueous pore, approximately pH 1-3; cooling and dilution after target formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis removes the alpha-carbamoyl group, generating arginine, carbon dioxide and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a selective prebiotic operation. Hydrolytic N-decarbamoylation is chemically grounded, but it must occur faster than damage to the side-chain guanidino group. No enzyme or modern protecting-group reagent is assumed. This selectivity and the required residence time are key unresolved constraints on every hydantoin-to-arginine branch.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"C4 aminonitrile guanidination before aldehyde formation\",\n      \"description\": \"Install the guanidino group on the C4 aminonitrile, partially reduce the remaining nitrile to an aldehyde, and complete Strecker extension followed by direct hydrolysis. This avoids guanidinating a molecule with two primary amines but requires unvalidated selective reduction of a guanidinated nitrile.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoaldehyde guanidination followed by direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate the aminoaldehyde, guanidinate its terminal amine, and then install and hydrolyze the alpha-aminonitrile. Competition between aminoaldehyde cyclization and guanidination is the distinguishing bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Ornithinonitrile guanidination followed by direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Perform Strecker extension before guanidination, then hydrolyze the guanidinated alpha-aminonitrile through argininamide. Side-chain versus alpha-amino guanidination is unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Ornithinamide-stage guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrate the ornithine precursor nitrile to its amide before guanidination, then hydrolyze argininamide. This changes the competing nitrile chemistry but still requires regioselective guanidination of an unprotected diamino compound.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker synthesis of ornithine followed by guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis before adding the guanidino group. This avoids exposing an already formed guanidino group to the initial hydrolysis sequence but leaves terminal guanidination selectivity unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoaldehyde guanidination followed by the arginine hydantoin branch\",\n      \"description\": \"Guanidinate before Strecker extension, trap the resulting alpha-aminonitrile as a hydantoin, and hydrolyze through N-alpha-carbamoylarginine. This substitutes a cyclic intermediate for direct nitrile-to-amide hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\", \"rxn_024\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Ornithinonitrile guanidination followed by hydantoin formation\",\n      \"description\": \"Guanidinate the Strecker-derived diamino nitrile before carbon dioxide capture and hydantoin formation. This requires both guanidination regioselectivity and guanidino-group survival through hydantoin processing.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\", \"rxn_024\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin-masked guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Convert ornithinonitrile to ornithine hydantoin before guanidination. The ring masks the alpha nitrogen and may improve side-chain selectivity; subsequent hydrolysis must preserve the newly installed guanidino group.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_023\", \"rxn_024\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Open-chain N-alpha-carbamoyl-masked guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Open ornithine hydantoin before guanidination, retaining the alpha-carbamoyl group as a temporary selectivity aid. Guanidination is followed only by decarbamoylation, avoiding alkaline hydantoin opening after guanidino-group installation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_024\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin route to ornithine followed by terminal guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Fully hydrolyze and decarbamoylate the ornithine hydantoin branch before guanidination. This avoids guanidino-group exposure to hydantoin-processing conditions but sacrifices the alpha-nitrogen masking advantage during the final reaction.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_016\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_019\",\n    \"mol_020\",\n    \"mol_021\",\n    \"mol_022\",\n    \"mol_023\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_020\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_024\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_024\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Arginine", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected, conditional reaction network containing 10 chemically distinct candidate pathways, not 10 experimentally established prebiotic syntheses. No end-to-end prebiotic synthesis of arginine is established by this network. Acetylene and HCN supply a four-carbon dinitrile skeleton; selective reduction would provide an aminoaldehyde for Strecker chain extension. Cyanamide supplies the guanidino carbon and two nitrogens. The pathways differ in when guanidination occurs and whether the alpha-amino-acid functionality is obtained through direct nitrile hydrolysis or a Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin branch. Hydantoins provide a chemically motivated opportunity to mask the alpha-amino group during side-chain guanidination. The shared, severe bottleneck is selective reduction of nitriles without over-reduction or cyclization; reactions rxn_003, rxn_004 and rxn_006 are explicitly speculative under the proposed geochemical conditions. Additional bottlenecks are aminoaldehyde cyclization, guanidination regioselectivity and survival during hydrolysis. Starting supplies of acetylene, HCN, ammonia, hydrogen, water, cyanamide and carbon dioxide are assumptions, not additional demonstrated synthesis pathways. All conditions exclude molecular oxygen, enzymes and modern activating reagents. Achiral chemistry predicts racemic arginine rather than selective L-arginine formation. Neutral formulas represent analytical species; solution protonation states depend on pH. Each environment_formed value identifies a representative formation environment, while reaction nodes specify alternative environments. The graph includes target-forming transformations, not every competing degradation product.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "Ethyne", "common_name": "Acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "Cyanamide", "common_name": "Cyanamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H3N", "iupac_name": "Prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "Acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H4N2", "iupac_name": "Butanedinitrile", "common_name": "Succinonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4N2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h1-2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H8N2", "iupac_name": "4-Aminobutanenitrile", "common_name": "Gamma-aminobutyronitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H9NO", "iupac_name": "4-Aminobutanal", "common_name": "Gamma-aminobutyraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H10N4", "iupac_name": "4-(Carbamimidamido)butanenitrile", "common_name": "4-Guanidinobutanenitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H11N3O", "iupac_name": "4-(Carbamimidamido)butanal", "common_name": "4-Guanidinobutanal", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H11N3", "iupac_name": "2,5-Diaminopentanenitrile", "common_name": "Ornithinonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H13N3O", "iupac_name": "2,5-Diaminopentanamide", "common_name": "Ornithinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H12N2O2", "iupac_name": "2,5-Diaminopentanoic acid", "common_name": "Ornithine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H12N2O2/c6-3-1-2-4(7)5(8)9/h4H,1-3,6-7H2,(H,8,9)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C6H13N5", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanenitrile", "common_name": "Argininonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C6H15N5O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanamide", "common_name": "Argininamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C6H11N3O2", "iupac_name": "5-(3-Aminopropyl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Ornithine hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C6H13N3O3", "iupac_name": "5-Amino-2-(carbamoylamino)pentanoic acid", "common_name": "N-alpha-Carbamoylornithine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C7H13N5O2", "iupac_name": "5-[3-(Carbamimidamido)propyl]imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Arginine hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C7H15N5O3", "iupac_name": "2-(Carbamoylamino)-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanoic acid", "common_name": "N-alpha-Carbamoylarginine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C6H14N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanoic acid", "common_name": "Arginine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Copper-catalyzed hydrocyanation of acetylene", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved copper(I) chloride complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper(I) chloride complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, chloride-rich, sulfide-poor acidic pool; sustained acetylene and HCN delivery; shaded reaction zone."}, "mechanism": "Copper-assisted addition of HCN across the acetylene triple bond produces acrylonitrile.", "reasoning": "Acetylene hydrocyanation is an established synthetic reaction class and supplies the required unsaturated nitrile without an oxygen oxidant. Its operation in a natural pool is speculative: catalyst concentration, gas retention and competing acetylene oligomerization are unresolved. Dissolved copper is proposed only in a sulfide-poor setting because sulfide would sequester it."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Conjugate hydrocyanation to the four-carbon dinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cyanide ions generated by HCN dissociation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cyanide ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Buffered pH approximately 8.5-10; concentrated but hydrated pool; limited residence time to reduce polymerization."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the beta carbon of acrylonitrile; proton transfer from HCN gives succinonitrile and regenerates cyanide.", "reasoning": "Conjugate cyanide addition to an activated alkene provides the correct unbranched C4 skeleton. The reaction class is chemically grounded, but useful production under these particular prebiotic conditions is not established. Acrylonitrile polymerization, hydrolysis and volatile-feedstock losses compete."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Selective hydrogenation of one succinonitrile nitrile group", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native nickel-iron alloy surface, proposed awaruite-like catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-420 K", "pressure": "50-200 bar total pressure; elevated hydrogen partial pressure", "catalyst": "Nickel-iron alloy surface; selectivity unvalidated", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic rock-water pore; restricted catalyst contact and hydrogen exposure; rapid removal of any mono-reduced product."}, "mechanism": "One nitrile undergoes hydrogenation through imine intermediates to a primary amine while the other nitrile remains intact.", "reasoning": "Speculative and a shared major bottleneck. Nickel can catalyze nitrile hydrogenation, and native nickel-iron alloys can occur in serpentinizing systems, but selective accumulation of this mono-reduced product on a natural alloy is not established. Over-reduction to putrescine and cyclization to five-membered nitrogen compounds could dominate. This reaction supplies the first terminal amino group."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Partial reduction and hydrolysis to 4-aminobutanal", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Nickel-bearing metal surface, hypothetical partial-reduction catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K", "pressure": "50-150 bar total pressure; limited hydrogen activity", "catalyst": "Nickel-bearing surface; required partial-reduction selectivity unvalidated", "aqueous": true, "other": "Water-rich, mildly acidic pore; short catalyst residence followed by cooling and aldehyde trapping."}, "mechanism": "Partial nitrile hydrogenation gives an aldimine, whose hydrolysis releases ammonia and the aldehyde.", "reasoning": "Speculative and potentially prohibitive. Nitrile-to-aldehyde chemistry requires stopping reduction at the imine and favoring hydrolysis over further hydrogenation; no suitable native-mineral selectivity is established here. The aminoaldehyde also strongly favors cyclic imine formation. This edge must not be treated as a demonstrated hydrothermal source of the Strecker substrate."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Guanidination before aminoaldehyde formation", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "325-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated, partially evaporated solution at pH approximately 9-10.5; residual water maintained; UV-shielded pore or sediment layer."}, "mechanism": "The terminal primary amine adds to the cyanamide nitrile carbon, followed by proton transfers to form a substituted guanidine.", "reasoning": "Cyanamide-mediated amine guanidination is chemically established, but this substrate-specific prebiotic application is speculative. Performing it before alpha-amino-group installation avoids competition between two primary amines. Competing aminonitrile cyclization and cyanamide dimerization may prevent useful conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Partial reduction of the guanidinated C4 nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Nickel-bearing metal surface, hypothetical partial-reduction catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K", "pressure": "50-150 bar total pressure; limited hydrogen activity", "catalyst": "Nickel-bearing surface; required chemoselectivity unvalidated", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly acidic, water-rich microenvironment; rapid product desorption and cooling."}, "mechanism": "The terminal nitrile is partially hydrogenated to an imine and hydrolyzed to an aldehyde, with ammonia release.", "reasoning": "Speculative. This alternative attempts to reduce aminoaldehyde cyclization by installing the less nucleophilic guanidino group first. It still lacks a demonstrated prebiotic catalyst that stops at the aldehyde, and guanidine adsorption could poison the metal or alter selectivity. Preservation of the guanidino group is an additional requirement."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Guanidination of 4-aminobutanal", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated cyanamide; pH approximately 8.5-10; freshly supplied aminoaldehyde; sheltered from direct UV."}, "mechanism": "The open-chain terminal amine adds to cyanamide to generate a guanidino substituent while retaining the aldehyde.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate application of established guanidination chemistry. Early guanidination avoids alpha-versus-side-chain amine competition later, but open-chain 4-aminobutanal may be only a minor species because of rapid five-membered cyclic imine formation. Aldehyde condensation and cyanamide side reactions are unresolved competitors."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Strecker extension to ornithinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia- and cyanide-containing pool, pH approximately 8-9.5; continuous aminoaldehyde supply; attenuated UV in sediment pores."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine with the aldehyde; cyanide addition installs the new carbon and alpha-amino functionality.", "reasoning": "Strecker chemistry is well established for suitable aldehydes, but efficient reaction of this unstable aminoaldehyde under the stated conditions is speculative. Intramolecular imine formation can outcompete intermolecular ammonia capture, and the product can undergo additional cyclization. No enantioselectivity is supplied."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Strecker extension of 4-guanidinobutanal", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich, cyanide-containing water at pH approximately 8-9.5; guanidino group predominantly protonated."}, "mechanism": "Aldehyde-imine formation followed by cyanide addition produces the alpha-aminonitrile carrying a preformed guanidino side chain.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically motivated but substrate-specific speculative Strecker reaction. Guanidinium protonation should reduce side-chain nucleophilicity relative to a free primary amine, potentially reducing cyclization. Actual aldehyde lifetime, competing condensation and aminonitrile yield remain unestablished."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Side-chain guanidination of ornithinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "325-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated cyanamide-containing solution; pH approximately 9-10.5; limited reaction time."}, "mechanism": "The terminal side-chain amine adds to cyanamide, generating the guanidino group without changing the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Speculative with respect to regioselectivity. Both the alpha-amino and terminal amino groups can react; their different electronic environments do not establish exclusive side-chain guanidination. The graph represents the target-forming product channel, not a predicted selective synthesis. Intramolecular nitrile capture and multiple guanidination are competing reactions."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydration of ornithinonitrile to ornithinamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K", "pressure": "10-100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic water, approximately pH 1-3; moderate residence time followed by cooling if amide accumulation is desired."}, "mechanism": "Acid-promoted water addition to the nitrile and proton transfers generate the primary carboxamide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydration is established chemistry, and protonation suppresses some amine-driven cyclization. Accumulating the amide rather than proceeding directly to the acid is a kinetic requirement, not an established environmental outcome. Product-specific rates and survival in acidic hydrothermal water require measurement."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Side-chain guanidination of ornithinamide", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "325-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cooling and pH adjustment after nitrile hydration; pH approximately 9-10.5; partial evaporative concentration."}, "mechanism": "The terminal amino group adds to cyanamide to form argininamide.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific application of cyanamide guanidination. Converting the nitrile to an amide changes electrophilicity and cyclization opportunities, making this a distinct chemical ordering. It does not protect the alpha-amino group, so wrong-site and double guanidination remain serious limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Hydrolysis of ornithinamide to ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-410 K", "pressure": "20-150 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic pore water, approximately pH 1-3; prolonged residence followed by cooling."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed carboxamide hydrolysis produces the amino acid and ammonia, predominantly present as ammonium at the reaction pH.", "reasoning": "Amide-to-acid hydrolysis is chemically well founded and completes the conventional Strecker sequence. The proposed setting supplies heat and acidity without oxygen or enzymes. Whether hydrolysis outpaces competing degradation at naturally attainable acid concentrations is uncertain."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Terminal guanidination of ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_023"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated solution at pH approximately 9-10.5; residual water during evaporation; protection from direct UV."}, "mechanism": "Addition of the ornithine side-chain amino group to cyanamide gives the arginine guanidino group.", "reasoning": "Cyanamide is a chemically credible guanyl donor, but selective conversion of unprotected ornithine under these geochemical conditions is not established here. Alpha-amino guanidination, multiple guanidination and cyanamide self-reaction compete. This is the shortest terminal assembly step, not a claim that arginine will be the dominant product."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Hydration of argininonitrile to argininamide", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K", "pressure": "10-100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic water at approximately pH 1-3; guanidino group protonated; controlled thermal exposure."}, "mechanism": "Acid-promoted nitrile hydration forms the primary amide while retaining the guanidino substituent.", "reasoning": "The functional-group transformation is established in general. Its useful rate and chemoselectivity on this substrate in a natural fluid remain speculative. Acidic conditions reduce nucleophilic side-chain reactions, but they do not guarantee quantitative guanidino-group survival."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Hydrolysis of argininamide to arginine", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-410 K", "pressure": "20-150 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic aqueous compartment, approximately pH 1-3; cooling after hydrolysis to limit further decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Carboxamide hydrolysis releases ammonia and produces the arginine carboxyl group.", "reasoning": "This uses ordinary amide hydrolysis rather than biological amidases. Arginine can survive some acidic hydrolysis conditions, but the balance between the required reaction time and substrate degradation must be established for the proposed fluid. No prebiotic yield is asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs capture of ornithinonitrile as a hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonium-carbonate-containing water at pH approximately 8-10; sufficient dissolved inorganic carbon; shaded concentrated pool."}, "mechanism": "Carbon dioxide capture by the alpha-amino group and subsequent cyclization and rearrangement give the hydantoin; this node represents the net Bucherer-Bergs conversion.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin formation through alpha-aminonitrile and inorganic-carbon chemistry is an established alternative to direct Strecker hydrolysis. Applying it selectively to this diamino substrate is speculative because the terminal amine also reacts and can promote cyclization. The hydantoin branch is included because it masks the alpha-amino group during later guanidination."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Guanidination of ornithine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "325-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated cyanamide; mildly alkaline water, approximately pH 8.5-10; avoid extended strong-base exposure."}, "mechanism": "The exocyclic primary amine adds to cyanamide, retaining the hydantoin ring as an alpha-amino-group mask.", "reasoning": "Speculative but chemically motivated: incorporation of the alpha nitrogen into a carbonyl-containing ring should reduce its competition with the terminal primary amine. Deprotonated hydantoin nitrogens may nevertheless react, and cyanamide self-condensation competes. Improved regioselectivity must be demonstrated rather than assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Ring-opening hydrolysis of ornithine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-430 K", "pressure": "50-200 bar", "catalyst": "Base-promoted hydrolysis by hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline water, approximately pH 10-11; prolonged residence may be necessary; subsequent cooling."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the hydantoin ring produces the N-alpha-carbamoyl amino acid.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin ring opening is established chemistry, but laboratory procedures can require harsher conditions than a natural alkaline vent supplies. Therefore useful conversion in this temperature and pH window is speculative. Accumulation of the open-chain carbamoyl compound creates a distinct site-protected guanidination opportunity."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Guanidination of N-alpha-carbamoylornithine", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "325-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cooled, concentrated solution at pH approximately 9-10.5; alpha-carbamoyl group retained during cyanamide exposure."}, "mechanism": "The free side-chain primary amine adds to cyanamide while the alpha nitrogen remains carbamoylated.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate application with a rational selectivity advantage: the carbamoylated alpha nitrogen is less nucleophilic than a free alpha amine. This avoids requiring cyanamide to discriminate between two unprotected primary amines. Carbamoyl stability and target-product selectivity under repeated concentration cycles remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Decarbamoylation to ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-420 K", "pressure": "30-150 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Transfer from alkaline water to an acidic pore, approximately pH 1-3; sufficient residence for carbamoyl cleavage."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group liberates ornithine; decomposition of the released carbamic-acid equivalent yields carbon dioxide and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Nonenzymatic removal of an N-carbamoyl group is chemically possible but often demanding. The proposed environmental rate is uncertain and may be too low before other degradation occurs. This completes the hydantoin route to ornithine without introducing a biological carbamoylase."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs capture of argininonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonium-carbonate-containing solution at pH approximately 8-10; guanidino side chain mostly protonated."}, "mechanism": "Carbon dioxide incorporation and cyclization of the alpha-aminonitrile generate the arginine-substituted hydantoin.", "reasoning": "This is a speculative substrate extension of established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. A protonated guanidino group should compete less strongly than a free terminal amine, but neither hydantoin yield nor side-chain survival is established under the proposed prebiotic conditions. The incorporated carbon is subsequently released during decarbamoylation."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Ring-opening hydrolysis of arginine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-430 K", "pressure": "50-200 bar", "catalyst": "Base-promoted hydrolysis by hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline water at approximately pH 10-11; thermal exposure minimized after ring opening."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis opens the hydantoin ring to N-alpha-carbamoylarginine.", "reasoning": "The ring-opening reaction class is established, but this substrate and geochemical operating window are speculative. A major selectivity problem is achieving hydantoin hydrolysis before guanidino-group hydrolysis or other decomposition. The graph does not assume that prolonged alkaline heating preserves arginine functionality."}, {"id": "rxn_024", "name": "Decarbamoylation to arginine", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-420 K", "pressure": "30-150 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic aqueous pore, approximately pH 1-3; cooling and dilution after target formation."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis removes the alpha-carbamoyl group, generating arginine, carbon dioxide and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Speculative as a selective prebiotic operation. Hydrolytic N-decarbamoylation is chemically grounded, but it must occur faster than damage to the side-chain guanidino group. No enzyme or modern protecting-group reagent is assumed. This selectivity and the required residence time are key unresolved constraints on every hydantoin-to-arginine branch."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "C4 aminonitrile guanidination before aldehyde formation", "description": "Install the guanidino group on the C4 aminonitrile, partially reduce the remaining nitrile to an aldehyde, and complete Strecker extension followed by direct hydrolysis. This avoids guanidinating a molecule with two primary amines but requires unvalidated selective reduction of a guanidinated nitrile.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_015", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Aminoaldehyde guanidination followed by direct Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Generate the aminoaldehyde, guanidinate its terminal amine, and then install and hydrolyze the alpha-aminonitrile. Competition between aminoaldehyde cyclization and guanidination is the distinguishing bottleneck.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_015", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Ornithinonitrile guanidination followed by direct hydrolysis", "description": "Perform Strecker extension before guanidination, then hydrolyze the guanidinated alpha-aminonitrile through argininamide. Side-chain versus alpha-amino guanidination is unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_015", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_014", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Ornithinamide-stage guanid