m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This is a connected, conditional reaction network containing 10 chemically distinct candidate pathways, not 10 experimentally established prebiotic syntheses. No end-to-end prebiotic synthesis of arginine is established by this network. Acetylene and HCN supply a four-carbon dinitrile skeleton; selective reduction would provide an aminoaldehyde for Strecker chain extension. Cyanamide supplies the guanidino carbon and two nitrogens. The pathways differ in when guanidination occurs and whether the alpha-amino-acid functionality is obtained through direct nitrile hydrolysis or a Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin branch. Hydantoins provide a chemically motivated opportunity to mask the alpha-amino group during side-chain guanidination. The shared, severe bottleneck is selective reduction of nitriles without over-reduction or cyclization; reactions rxn_003, rxn_004 and rxn_006 are explicitly speculative under the proposed geochemical conditions. Additional bottlenecks are aminoaldehyde cyclization, guanidination regioselectivity and survival during hydrolysis. Starting supplies of acetylene, HCN, ammonia, hydrogen, water, cyanamide and carbon dioxide are assumptions, not additional demonstrated synthesis pathways. All conditions 