_progression": 0.3, "target_achievement": 0.16, "overall_confidence": 0.29, "feedback": "Hydantoin formation occurs after guanidination, so it cannot prevent or correct wrong-site guanidination in rxn_010. This pathway retains the speculative nitrile reductions, cyclic-imine competition during rxn_008 and unprotected diamine selectivity problem. The hydantoin branch is formally balanced and chemically recognizable, but its ring-opening and decarbamoylation conditions are unspecified and target recovery is unverified.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_023", "rxn_024"], "suggestions": ["Establish the structure and regioisomeric purity of argininonitrile before testing hydantoin formation.", "Measure hydantoin conversion and subsequent arginine recovery under a fully specified, nonenzymatic condition sequence.", "Compare guanidination before and after alpha-amino masking experimentally rather than assuming later hydantoin formation resolves earlier selectivity."]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.62, "rxn_002": 0.7, "rxn_003": 0.2, "rxn_004": 0.1, "rxn_008": 0.32, "rxn_017": 0.58, "rxn_018": 0.64, "rxn_023": 0.55, "rxn_024": 0.42}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.34, "pathway_coherence": 0.7, "environmental_progression": 0.31, "target_achievement": 0.21, "overall_confidence": 0.35, "feedback": "This pathway has a sound late-stage selectivity rationale: hydantoin formation reduces alpha-amino nucleophilicity before side-chain guanidination. That is a chemical advantage, not experimental proof of selectivity. Rxn_017 must give the intended hydantoin despite the additional amino group, and rxn_023/rxn_024 must subsequently recover arginine. Most importantly, masking does not bypass rxn_003/rxn_004 or the competition between open-chain and cyclic Strecker chemistry in rxn_008.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_017", "rxn_023", "rxn_024"], "suggestions": ["Demonstrate ornithine hydantoin formation under specified ammonia/CO2 conditions and identify competing cyclic or carbamoylated products.", "Measure side-chain versus hydantoin-nitrogen guanidination and the stability of the mask under rxn_018 conditions.", "Validate nonenzymatic deprotection through rxn_024 while separately establishing the upstream selective reductions and ornithinonitrile formation."]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.62, "rxn_002": 0.7, "rxn_003": 0.2, "rxn_004": 0.1, "rxn_008": 0.32, "rxn_017": 0.58, "rxn_019": 0.56, "rxn_020": 0.62, "rxn_024": 0.42}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.33, "pathway_coherence": 0.68, "environmental_progression": 0.31, "target_achievement": 0.2, "overall_confidence": 0.34, "feedback": "An N-alpha-carbamoyl group plausibly reduces alpha-nitrogen reactivity and improves the rationale for side-chain guanidination. However, rxn_019 must accumulate the intended open-chain intermediate, and that mask must survive rxn_020 without substantial reclosure or premature removal. Final decarbamoylation to arginine remains condition-dependent and unverified. The shared selective reductions and aminoaldehyde cyclization problem are unchanged.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_017", "rxn_019", "rxn_024"], "suggestions": ["Confirm the identity and accumulation of N-alpha-carbamoylornithine during hydantoin ring opening.", "Measure mask retention, hydantoin reclosure and guanidination regioselectivity under rxn_020 conditions.", "Establish a nonenzymatic decarbamoylation window that releases arginine faster than competing decomposition."]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.62, "rxn_002": 0.7, "rxn_003": 0.2, "rxn_004": 0.1, "rxn_008": 0.32, "rxn_017": 0.58, "rxn_019": 0.56, "rxn_021": 0.44, "rxn_014": 0.38}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.29, "pathway_coherence": 0.59, "environmental_progression": 0.3, "target_achievement": 0.17, "overall_confidence": 0.3, "feedback": "The hydantoin branch provides a formally valid route to ornithine, but removing the alpha-amino mask before rxn_014 forfeits its potential benefit for guanidination selectivity. Terminal guanidination therefore has the same unresolved two-amine problem as P5. Nonenzymatic hydantoin opening and decarbamoylation also require demonstrated conditions. None of these transformations bypasses the speculative reductions or competing cyclic chemistry before ornithinonitrile formation.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_017", "rxn_019", "rxn_021", "rxn_014"], "suggestions": ["Demonstrate ornithine recovery through rxn_019 and rxn_021 under realistic hydrothermal pH and residence times.", "Quantify side-chain-selective guanidination of the recovered ornithine, including alternative constitutional isomers.", "Compare terminal guanidination with guanidination while the alpha-amino mask remains present; retain only strategies supported by measured selectivity."]}], "best_pathway_id": "P1", "overall_confidence": 0.34, "network_feedback": "The network has strong structural validity: it contains sufficient nodes, is connected, uses molecule IDs consistently and provides formal routes to arginine. The supplied deterministic balance check is accepted as ground truth. There are no conservation violations; rxn_003 requires 2 H2, a reasonable stoichiometric correction. Carbon and nitrogen accounting is coherent. Chemical viability is much weaker. Every target route requires rxn_003 followed by either rxn_004 or rxn_006, and none has a demonstrated geochemical catalyst and operating window for the required selectivity. Over-reduction and cyclization are substantial alternatives. Routes through free 4-aminobutanal additionally face cyclic-imine formation, while unmasked guanidination routes require unresolved positional selectivity. Hydantoin and carbamoyl masking are chemically motivated but do not establish upstream precursor production or successful nonenzymatic deprotection. Environmental scores reflect missing reaction-specific information: temperature, pH, H2 pressure, catalyst identity and availability, residence time, and physically justified cooling and transfer. A copper-catalyst label does not establish an active natural copper phase or suitable cyanide speciation. No penalty is assigned for the number or ordering of environments. Starting supplies are accepted as stipulated, and racemic arginine satisfies the stated target. The explicit acknowledgment of speculation is appropriate but does not substitute for evidence. No research findings were supplied for a separate literature-alignment assessment. P1 is the best conditional design because it avoids unprotected aminoaldehyde handling and later two-amine guanidination, not because its key reductions are established. Confidence is bottleneck-weighted rather than an average of reaction scores. The complete network is not approved."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 290413.1829738617, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:04:03.042096", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Arginine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.34\n- Feedback: The network has strong structural validity: it contains sufficient nodes, is connected, uses molecule IDs consistently and provides formal routes to arginine. The supplied deterministic balance check is accepted as ground truth. There are no conservation violations; rxn_003 requires 2 H2, a reasonable stoichiometric correction. Carbon and nitrogen accounting is coherent. Chemical viability is much weaker. Every target route requires rxn_003 followed by either rxn_004 or rxn_006, and none has a demonstrated geochemical catalyst and operating window for the required selectivity. Over-reduction and cyclization are substantial alternatives. Routes through free 4-aminobutanal additionally face cyclic-imine formation, while unmasked guanidination routes require unresolved positional selectivity. Hydantoin and carbamoyl masking are chemically motivated but do not establish upstream precursor production or successful nonenzymatic deprotection. Environmental scores reflect missing reaction-specific information: temperature, pH, H2 pressure, catalyst identity and availability, residence time, and physically justified cooling and transfer. A copper-catalyst label does not establish an active natural copper phase or suitable cyanide speciation. No penalty is assigned for the number or ordering of environments. Starting supplies are accepted as stipulated, and racemic arginine satisfies the stated target. The explicit acknowledgment of speculation is appropriate but does not substitute for evidence. No research findings were supplied for a separate literature-alignment assessment. P1 is the best conditional design because it avoids unprotected aminoaldehyde handling and later two-amine guanidination, not because its key reductions are established. Confidence is bottleneck-weighted rather than an average of reaction scores. The complete network is not approved.\n- Issues: ['This is the strongest conditional design: early guanidination avoids competition between alpha and side-chain amino groups and may suppress the cyclization associated with an unprotected aminoaldehyde. Nevertheless, rxn_003 and rxn_006 require two different, unestablished hydrogenation selectivities. An unspecified hydrothermal catalyst cannot be assumed to stop at the required aminonitrile or aldehyde. The subsequent Strecker and hydrolysis sequence is formally credible, but production of its aldehyde substrate and survival of the guanidino group remain undemonstrated.', 'Guanidination before introducing the alpha-amino group avoids a later two-amine regioselectivity problem. However, rxn_004 must produce 4-aminobutanal without over-reduction, and rxn_007 must capture it despite favorable five-membered cyclic imine formation. Cyanamide guanidination cannot be assumed to outcompete this intramolecular reaction. The guanidinated-aldehyde Strecker sequence is reasonable in principle, but its precursor supply remains unsupported.', 'Besides the speculative reductions, rxn_008 must favor intermolecular Strecker chemistry over aminoaldehyde cyclization. Cyanide capture of the resulting cyclic imine can instead lead toward prolinenitrile. Rxn_010 then requires side-chain-selective guanidination of an unprotected diamino substrate; neither the molecular formula nor the balanced equation establishes that selectivity. The final hydrolyses are recognizable transformations, but they cannot correct an incorrectly cyclized or guanidinated precursor.', 'Hydration to ornithinamide is chemically plausible, but it does not protect the alpha-amino group. Rxn_012 therefore still requires experimentally demonstrated side-chain selectivity. Accumulating the amide for guanidination also requires control over continued hydrolysis and competing cyclization. The common rxn_003/rxn_004 reduction bottleneck and rxn_008 aminoaldehyde competition remain unresolved, so this is a formal route rather than an established source of arginine.', 'The aminonitrile-to-ornithine hydrolysis sequence is conventional in outline. Its accessibility is nevertheless limited by rxn_003, rxn_004 and competition with cyclic products in rxn_008. Terminal cyanamide guanidination of unprotected ornithine is not automatically selective for the side-chain amino group. The final equation identifies the intended product but provides no evidence distinguishing arginine from alternative guanidination products.', 'Bucherer-Bergs capture is a plausible reaction-family extension, provided suitable aqueous CO2, ammonia and carbonate speciation is established. It does not solve the earlier selective reductions or the need for rxn_007 to outcompete aminoaldehyde cyclization. Because guanidination has already occurred, the hydantoin is not providing selectivity during that step. Nonenzymatic ring opening and decarbamoylation must be shown to release arginine while retaining its guanidino group.', 'Hydantoin formation occurs after guanidination, so it cannot prevent or correct wrong-site guanidination in rxn_010. This pathway retains the speculative nitrile reductions, cyclic-imine competition during rxn_008 and unprotected diamine selectivity problem. The hydantoin branch is formally balanced and chemically recognizable, but its ring-opening and decarbamoylation conditions are unspecified and target recovery is unverified.', 'This pathway has a sound late-stage selectivity rationale: hydantoin formation reduces alpha-amino nucleophilicity before side-chain guanidination. That is a chemical advantage, not experimental proof of selectivity. Rxn_017 must give the intended hydantoin despite the additional amino group, and rxn_023/rxn_024 must subsequently recover arginine. Most importantly, masking does not bypass rxn_003/rxn_004 or the competition between open-chain and cyclic Strecker chemistry in rxn_008.', 'An N-alpha-carbamoyl group plausibly reduces alpha-nitrogen reactivity and improves the rationale for side-chain guanidination. However, rxn_019 must accumulate the intended open-chain intermediate, and that mask must survive rxn_020 without substantial reclosure or premature removal. Final decarbamoylation to arginine remains condition-dependent and unverified. The shared selective reductions and aminoaldehyde cyclization problem are unchanged.', 'The hydantoin branch provides a formally valid route to ornithine, but removing the alpha-amino mask before rxn_014 forfeits its potential benefit for guanidination selectivity. Terminal guanidination therefore has the same unresolved two-amine problem as P5. Nonenzymatic hydantoin opening and decarbamoylation also require demonstrated conditions. None of these transformations bypasses the speculative reductions or competing cyclic chemistry before ornithinonitrile formation.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine, racemic\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This is a connected, conditional synthesis network, not ten experimentally established prebiotic syntheses. Ten distinct graph pathways combine two cyanamide-source channels with five guanyl-transfer routes. The common carbon-skeleton route uses a nitro group as a latent terminal amine: nitromethane addition to acrolein, Strecker chemistry, complete nitrile hydrolysis, alpha-amino acetylation, and complete nitro reduction. This avoids selective partial nitrile hydrogenation and free 4-aminobutanal. Acetylating the only amino group before reducing the nitro group avoids assuming selective guanidination of unprotected ornithine. Final acidic deacetylation replaces hydantoin or carbamoyl deprotection. Important unresolved bottlenecks remain: substrate-specific nitro-Michael selectivity, precursor availability, native-iron reduction performance, and deacetylation within the stated environmental window. The network therefore cannot be presented as an approved or demonstrated complete geochemical synthesis. Feedstock inventories are stipulated, particularly acrolein, nitromethane, cyanogen, and concentrated ammonia. Environments are separate reaction compartments, not a single compatible mixture. All reactions are anoxic. Molecular formulas generally represent neutral reference structures rather than every pH-dependent protonation state. Pathway sequences are topologically ordered preparation schedules and include preparation of converging reactants.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3