Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_031\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acetic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c1-2(3)4/h1H3,(H,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_032\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-(diaminomethylideneamino)pentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine, racemic\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Metal-sulfide-mediated methyl thioacetate formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Freshly precipitated FeS/NiS catalytic assemblage\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-385 K; laboratory precedent is near 373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar total in the proposed shallow subsurface setting; CO partial pressure approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic CO-bearing, methanethiol-bearing water. The higher total-pressure setting is an extrapolation. Thioester must leave the reaction zone before extensive hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-sulfide-mediated carbonyl incorporation and sulfur-containing acetyl transfer produce a thioester. The inferred net stoichiometry consumes two methanethiol molecules per carbon monoxide molecule.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Activated acetate formation from CO and methanethiol on Fe/Ni sulfides has experimental precedent (Huber and Wachtershauser, 1997). This supports the reaction family, not efficient operation in an arbitrary vent. A short shallow-circulation connection to cooler surface water is required; survival during transport and subsequent sulfide depletion remain unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitro-Michael construction of the four-carbon aldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved carbonate/bicarbonate acting as a general base\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Carbonate/bicarbonate general-base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 9-10, nitromethane in excess, low instantaneous acrolein concentration, shaded pore water, and hours-to-days residence time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitromethane deprotonation generates a nitronate that adds at the beta carbon of acrolein; protonation gives 4-nitrobutanal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific prebiotic implementation of established nitro-Michael chemistry. The nitro group provides a latent terminal amine without introducing an aminoaldehyde. However, competing Henry addition at the aldehyde, acrolein polymerization, and product aldol chemistry must be measured. This is a shared, potentially decisive bottleneck, not an established selective precursor source.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker capture of 4-nitrobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7.5-8.5 with concentrated total ammonia/ammonium and retained HCN; hours-to-days residence time. Acid-base adjustment follows the preceding alkaline reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine or iminium species at the aldehyde, followed by cyanide addition to generate a racemic alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker chemistry is well established, but this exact nitroaldehyde under the proposed conditions requires experimental validation. Unlike 4-aminobutanal, the substrate lacks a terminal amine capable of rapid cyclic-imine formation. Aldehyde oligomerization and cyanohydrin accumulation remain competitors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Alpha-aminonitrile hydration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium from chloride-rich acidic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"345-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-3 mol/L strong acid, covered or continuously replenished acidic pools, and days-to-weeks residence time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed water addition to the protonated nitrile produces the corresponding primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydration is conventional chemistry and avoids any requirement to partially hydrogenate a nitrile. The nitro group remains a latent amine. Reaction-specific kinetics and nitro-group survival have not been established here; strongly acidic water of this concentration is itself a conditional local inventory.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete amide hydrolysis to the nitro amino acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium from chloride-rich acidic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"355-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-3 mol/L strong acid and weeks-to-months residence time. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis replaces the carboxamide nitrogen with hydroxyl, releasing ammonia after protonation-state normalization.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a recognizable hydrolysis, but adequate conversion below 370 K cannot be assumed. It may be slower than initial nitrile hydration and is explicitly included as a kinetic bottleneck. Reduction is postponed until the nitrile-derived carbon has become a carboxyl group.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Selective alpha-amino acetylation before terminal-amine unveiling\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated solution at proposed pH 8-9 following neutralization of the hydrolysis mixture. Prompt thioester capture is required; extensive drying is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The sole amino group attacks methyl thioacetate, producing an N-acetyl amide and methanethiol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thioester aminolysis supplies a chemically motivated protecting step without assuming discrimination between two free amino groups. The terminal nitrogen remains a nitro group and cannot undergo ordinary amine acetylation. Exact conversion is speculative because thioester hydrolysis competes and the proposed substrate has not been validated in this environmental sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete nitro reduction using native iron\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Fresh native or meteoritic Fe0 grains, consumed as a stoichiometric reductant\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm; no imposed hydrogen pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-3, chloride-bearing water, freshly exposed iron surfaces, low dissolved sulfide, and hours-to-days contact. Competing corrosion and hydrogen evolution consume reductant.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A net six-electron nitro-to-amine reduction proceeds through nitroso and hydroxylamine oxidation levels. Stoichiometry requires three Fe0 and six protons per substrate, yielding three Fe2+ and two waters.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Unlike the previous selective hydrogenation design, this step seeks complete reduction of one nitro group after nitrile hydrolysis. The alpha nitrogen is already acylated, preventing ordinary aminoaldehyde-type cyclization. Acidic metal reduction is chemically credible, but native-iron performance on this exact substrate is unverified. Hydroxylamine accumulation, passivation, and acetyl-mask survival must be measured; a generic iron label alone does not establish viability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional discharge source of cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K bulk collection environment; transient discharge plasma is much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic methane/ammonia-containing gas exposed to electrical discharge, followed by prompt collection into water. This is not a 300-330 K thermal reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A coarse-grained radical and ion chemistry channel incorporates methane carbon and ammonia nitrogen into cyanamide while eliminating hydrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Primitive-Earth simulation production of cyanamide has historical precedent (Schimpl, Lemmon and Calvin, 1965), but that does not validate this exact simplified feed composition or net channel. This node is explicitly speculative and is not an elementary reaction or a claimed clean conversion. If the temperature constraint excludes transient plasma chemistry, pathways using this node are outside that stricter interpretation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanogen ammonolysis to cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich water, proposed pH 8-9, low cyanogen inventory, and rapid capture after gas dissolution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia attacks cyanogen, with cyanide displacement giving cyanamide and HCN after proton transfer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanogen cleavage by ammonia provides a chemically distinct cyanamide source without a hydrogenation catalyst. Its environmental implementation remains conditional: cyanogen supply is stipulated, hydrolysis competes, and actual cyanamide accumulation must be demonstrated. This route should not be interpreted as evidence that cyanogen was abundant in surface pools.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Methanol capture of cyanamide as O-methylisourea\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen chloride acting as a protonating acid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydrogen chloride\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Methanol-rich, low-water acidic pore liquid; product is initially present as an isourea salt. Volatile retention and subsequent pH adjustment are required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pinner-type alcohol addition to protonated cyanamide produces an O-methylisourea framework.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a recognizable laboratory activation chemistry, but its prebiotic implementation is weakly supported. Low water activity, substantial methanol, and strong acid must coexist without rapid volatilization or hydrolysis. It is included as a conditional alternative, not as an established evaporitic-pool process.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanol capture of cyanamide as O-ethylisourea\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen chloride acting as a protonating acid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydrogen chloride\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Ethanol-rich, low-water acidic liquid; isourea salt formation precedes transfer into an alkaline aqueous reaction compartment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-assisted ethanol addition to the cyanamide nitrile carbon forms O-ethylisourea.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The chemical transformation follows the same established activation family as the methyl analogue, but ethanol availability and low-water acid chemistry are independent environmental constraints. This branch is speculative geochemically and offers no demonstrated advantage unless its feedstock and persistence are locally superior.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Methanethiol addition to cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_028\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium as an electrophile-activating acid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with replenished methanethiol vapor\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-3, concentrated cyanamide, and continuous thiol delivery from a nearby sulfur-bearing gas source. Evaporation alone cannot retain methanethiol.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thiol attack on protonated cyanamide generates an S-methylisothiourea framework after proton transfer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically plausible sulfur analogue of alcohol capture, but accumulation in the stated aqueous window is speculative. Methanethiol volatility, cyanamide hydrolysis, and actual addition kinetics are unresolved. The sulfur reagent is explicit rather than attributed to an unspecified mineral catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanethiol addition to cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_025\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_029\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium as an electrophile-activating acid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-308 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-3, sustained ethanethiol supply, and sheltered liquid interfaces limiting gas loss.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ethanethiol adds to activated cyanamide to produce S-ethylisothiourea.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative environmental implementation of a plausible thiol-addition route. Ethanethiol is less volatile than methanethiol but still requires a demonstrated source and retention mechanism. No prebiotic yield or dominance over cyanamide hydrolysis is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct cyanamide guanidination of alpha-protected ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 9.5-10.5, concentrated reactants, and days-to-weeks residence time. Keep the substrate hydrated to limit lactamization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal primary amine adds to cyanamide, followed by proton transfers that generate the guanidino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amine guanidination by cyanamide is chemically established, but this exact protected substrate and environmental window require verification. Crucially, the alpha nitrogen is an amide before the terminal amine is unveiled; side-chain selectivity is therefore chemically motivated rather than assumed for an unprotected diamine. Cyanamide dimerization, hydrolysis, and slow reaction remain competitors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"O-methylisourea-mediated guanidination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 10-11 following neutralization of the isourea-forming compartment; hours-to-days reaction time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The unmasked terminal amine attacks the isourea carbon, displacing methanol after proton transfer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"O-alkylisoureas are established guanyl-transfer reagents. Alpha acetylation removes the principal two-amine regioselectivity problem, but neither reagent production nor reaction yield under these natural conditions is established. Hydrolysis of the donor can consume it before productive capture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"O-ethylisourea-mediated guanidination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_027\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 10-11, concentrated protected ornithine, and hours-to-days residence time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Terminal-amine substitution at the isourea carbon gives the protected guanidino amino acid and ethanol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically distinct guanyl donor within a known reaction family. It does not require selective reaction of two free amines. Its environmental merit depends on actual O-ethylisourea availability and lifetime; these have not been demonstrated for the proposed setting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"S-methylisothiourea-mediated guanidination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_028\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8.5-10, hours-to-days residence time, and gas exchange permitting released methanethiol to escape.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amine replaces the methylthio group at the electrophilic guanyl carbon, releasing methanethiol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"S-alkylisothiourea guanyl transfer is conventional synthetic chemistry and avoids an unspecified copper catalyst. Protection of the alpha amine supplies a real selectivity rationale. The weak point is environmental production and persistence of the donor, not a demonstrated prebiotic reaction sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"S-ethylisothiourea-mediated guanidination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_029\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_025\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8.5-10, concentrated protected ornithine, and hours-to-days reaction time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic guanyl transfer from S-ethylisothiourea to the terminal amine releases ethanethiol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The bond-forming chemistry is supported at the reaction-family level, while this substrate-specific environmental implementation remains speculative. No claim is made that the ethyl donor is more prebiotically abundant or more effective than the methyl donor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic alpha-deacetylation to release arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_032\", \"mol_031\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium from concentrated acidic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"355-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-6 mol/L strong acid and days-to-weeks or longer residence time. Sustained acid concentration requires a specific volcanic-condensate setting; final neutralization changes protonation state but not molecular connectivity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of the alpha N-acetyl amide releases acetate/acetic acid and the alpha amino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ordinary amide hydrolysis provides a stronger chemical basis than assuming mild spontaneous hydantoin or carbamoyl deprotection. Acidic rather than strongly alkaline treatment is chosen to reduce the risk of guanidino-group destruction. Nevertheless, adequate deacetylation at or below 370 K while retaining arginine must be demonstrated experimentally. This remains a shared endpoint bottleneck; authentic arginine standards and structural analysis, not formula matching alone, are required.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-derived cyanamide with direct guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Prepare the protected ornithine skeleton, obtain cyanamide through the conditional discharge channel, guanidinate directly, and hydrolyze the acetyl mask. This minimizes donor activation steps but inherits the uncertain discharge source.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanogen-derived cyanamide with direct guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Use cyanogen ammonolysis to supply cyanamide for direct reaction with alpha-protected ornithine. This is the least elaborated non-discharge candidate, conditional on cyanogen availability and validation of the shared skeleton and deprotection steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge cyanamide through O-methylisourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Convert discharge-derived cyanamide into O-methylisourea before guanyl transfer to the protected amino acid. Low-water methanolic acid chemistry is an additional environmental bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_026\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanogen cyanamide through O-methylisourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine cyanogen ammonolysis with methanol-based guanyl-donor activation, protected side-chain guanidination, and final deacetylation. No step is claimed to establish the entire environmental sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_026\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge cyanamide through O-ethylisourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Use ethanol capture of discharge-derived cyanamide to form an alternative guanyl donor. The route requires separate acidic low-water and alkaline aqueous compartments.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanogen cyanamide through O-ethylisourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate cyanamide by cyanogen ammonolysis and activate it with ethanol before protected guanidination. Its distinctness concerns donor chemistry and precursor provenance, not an independent solution to the common bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge cyanamide through S-methylisothiourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Capture discharge-derived cyanamide with methanethiol, then transfer the guanyl group to the sole unmasked amine. Thiol availability and donor accumulation require experimental confirmation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_028\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanogen cyanamide through S-methylisothiourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine cyanogen ammonolysis, methanethiol-based donor formation, protected guanidination, and acid deacetylation. This avoids plasma chemistry but retains an unverified aqueous thiol-addition step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_028\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge cyanamide through S-ethylisothiourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Use ethanethiol to prepare a sulfur-based guanyl donor from discharge-derived cyanamide. This is a conditional alternative for a setting with an independently justified ethanethiol supply.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_029\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanogen cyanamide through S-ethylisothiourea\",\n      \"description\": \"Supply cyanamide by cyanogen ammonolysis, form the ethyl sulfur donor, guanidinate alpha-protected ornithine, and remove the acetyl group. Like the other nine variants, this is a testable hypothesis rather than a demonstrated prebiotic synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_029\", \"mol_030\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_030\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_030\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_019\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Arginine, racemic", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected, conditional synthesis network, not ten experimentally established prebiotic syntheses. Ten distinct graph pathways combine two cyanamide-source channels with five guanyl-transfer routes. The common carbon-skeleton route uses a nitro group as a latent terminal amine: nitromethane addition to acrolein, Strecker chemistry, complete nitrile hydrolysis, alpha-amino acetylation, and complete nitro reduction. This avoids selective partial nitrile hydrogenation and free 4-aminobutanal. Acetylating the only amino group before reducing the nitro group avoids assuming selective guanidination of unprotected ornithine. Final acidic deacetylation replaces hydantoin or carbamoyl deprotection. Important unresolved bottlenecks remain: substrate-specific nitro-Michael selectivity, precursor availability, native-iron reduction performance, and deacetylation within the stated environmental window. The network therefore cannot be presented as an approved or demonstrated complete geochemical synthesis. Feedstock inventories are stipulated, particularly acrolein, nitromethane, cyanogen, and concentrated ammonia. Environments are separate reaction compartments, not a single compatible mixture. All reactions are anoxic. Molecular formulas generally represent neutral reference structures rather than every pH-dependent protonation state. Pathway sequences are topologically ordered preparation schedules and include preparation of converging reactants.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C3H4O", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enal", "common_name": "Acrolein", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CH3NO2", "iupac_name": "nitromethane", "common_name": "Nitromethane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH3NO2/c1-2(3)4/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H7NO3", "iupac_name": "4-nitrobutanal", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C5H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-nitropentanenitrile", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H11N3O3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-nitropentanamide", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H10N2O4", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-nitropentanoic acid", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "Carbon monoxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CH4S", "iupac_name": "methanethiol", "common_name": "Methanethiol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4S/c1-2/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H6OS", "iupac_name": "S-methyl ethanethioate", "common_name": "Methyl thioacetate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6OS/c1-3(4)5-2/h1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C7H12N2O5", "iupac_name": "2-acetamido-5-nitropentanoic acid", "common_name": "N-alpha-acetyl-2-amino-5-nitropentanoic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "Native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "Proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "Fe2+", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C7H14N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-acetamido-5-aminopentanoic acid", "common_name": "N-alpha-acetylornithine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "Methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C2N2", "iupac_name": "ethanedinitrile", "common_name": "Cyanogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2N2/c3-1-2-4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "cyanamide", "common_name": "Cyanamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "CH4O", "iupac_name": "methanol", "common_name": "Methanol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4O/c1-2/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "C2H6O", "iupac_name": "ethanol", "common_name": "Ethanol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6O/c1-2-3/h3H,2H2,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "C2H6S", "iupac_name": "ethanethiol", "common_name": "Ethanethiol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6S/c1-2-3/h3H,2H2,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "methyl carbamimidate", "common_name": "O-methylisourea", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_027", "formula": "C3H8N2O", "iupac_name": "ethyl carbamimidate", "common_name": "O-ethylisourea", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_028", "formula": "C2H6N2S", "iupac_name": "methyl carbamimidothioate", "common_name": "S-methylisothiourea", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_029", "formula": "C3H8N2S", "iupac_name": "ethyl carbamimidothioate", "common_name": "S-ethylisothiourea", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_030", "formula": "C8H16N4O3", "iupac_name": "2-acetamido-5-(diaminomethylideneamino)pentanoic acid", "common_name": "N-alpha-acetylarginine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_031", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "ethanoic acid", "common_name": "Acetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c1-2(3)4/h1H3,(H,2,3)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_032", "formula": "C6H14N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-(diaminomethylideneamino)pentanoic acid", "common_name": "Arginine, racemic", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Metal-sulfide-mediated methyl thioacetate formation", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Freshly precipitated FeS/NiS catalytic assemblage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-385 K; laboratory precedent is near 373 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar total in the proposed shallow subsurface setting; CO partial pressure approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "FeS/NiS", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic CO-bearing, methanethiol-bearing water. The higher total-pressure setting is an extrapolation. Thioester must leave the reaction zone before extensive hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Metal-sulfide-mediated carbonyl incorporation and sulfur-containing acetyl transfer produce a thioester. The inferred net stoichiometry consumes two methanethiol molecules per carbon monoxide molecule.", "reasoning": "Activated acetate formation from CO and methanethiol on Fe/Ni sulfides has experimental precedent (Huber and Wachtershauser, 1997). This supports the reaction family, not efficient operation in an arbitrary vent. A short shallow-circulation connection to cooler surface water is required; survival during transport and subsequent sulfide depletion remain unverified."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Nitro-Michael construction of the four-carbon aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved carbonate/bicarbonate acting as a general base"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Carbonate/bicarbonate general-base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 9-10, nitromethane in excess, low instantaneous acrolein concentration, shaded pore water, and hours-to-days residence time."}, "mechanism": "Nitromethane deprotonation generates a nitronate that adds at the beta carbon of acrolein; protonation gives 4-nitrobutanal.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific prebiotic implementation of established nitro-Michael chemistry. The nitro group provides a latent terminal amine without introducing an aminoaldehyde. However, competing Henry addition at the aldehyde, acrolein polymerization, and product aldol chemistry must be measured. This is a shared, potentially decisive bottleneck, not an established selective precursor source."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Strecker capture of 4-nitrobutanal", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7.5-8.5 with concentrated total ammonia/ammonium and retained HCN; hours-to-days residence time. Acid-base adjustment follows the preceding alkaline reaction."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine or iminium species at the aldehyde, followed by cyanide addition to generate a racemic alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Strecker chemistry is well established, but this exact nitroaldehyde under the proposed conditions requires experimental validation. Unlike 4-aminobutanal, the substrate lacks a terminal amine capable of rapid cyclic-imine formation. Aldehyde oligomerization and cyanohydrin accumulation remain competitors."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Alpha-aminonitrile hydration", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium from chloride-rich acidic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "345-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed 1-3 mol/L strong acid, covered or continuously replenished acidic pools, and days-to-weeks residence time."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed water addition to the protonated nitrile produces the corresponding primary amide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydration is conventional chemistry and avoids any requirement to partially hydrogenate a nitrile. The nitro group remains a latent amine. Reaction-specific kinetics and nitro-group survival have not been established here; strongly acidic water of this concentration is itself a conditional local inventory."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Complete amide hydrolysis to the nitro amino acid", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium from chloride-rich acidic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "355-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed 1-3 mol/L strong acid and weeks-to-months residence time. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis replaces the carboxamide nitrogen with hydroxyl, releasing ammonia after protonation-state normalization.", "reasoning": "This is a recognizable hydrolysis, but adequate conversion below 370 K cannot be assumed. It may be slower than initial nitrile hydration and is explicitly included as a kinetic bottleneck. Reduction is postponed until the nitrile-derived carbon has become a carboxyl group."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Selective alpha-amino acetylation before terminal-amine unveiling", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-335 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated solution at proposed pH 8-9 following neutralization of the hydrolysis mixture. Prompt thioester capture is required; extensive drying is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "The sole amino group attacks methyl thioacetate, producing an N-acetyl amide and methanethiol.", "reasoning": "Thioester aminolysis supplies a chemically motivated protecting step without assuming discrimination between two free amino groups. The terminal nitrogen remains a nitro group and cannot undergo ordinary amine acetylation. Exact conversion is speculative because thioester hydrolysis competes and the proposed substrate has not been validated in this environmental sequence."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Complete nitro reduction using native iron", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_017", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fresh native or meteoritic Fe0 grains, consumed as a stoichiometric reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm; no imposed hydrogen pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-3, chloride-bearing water, freshly exposed iron surfaces, low dissolved sulfide, and hours-to-days contact. Competing corrosion and hydrogen evolution consume reductant."}, "mechanism": "A net six-electron nitro-to-amine reduction proceeds through nitroso and hydroxylamine oxidation levels. Stoichiometry requires three Fe0 and six protons per substrate, yielding three Fe2+ and two waters.", "reasoning": "Unlike the previous selective hydrogenation design, this step seeks complete reduction of one nitro group after nitrile hydrolysis. The alpha nitrogen is already acylated, preventing ordinary aminoaldehyde-type cyclization. Acidic metal reduction is chemically credible, but native-iron performance on this exact substrate is unverified. Hydroxylamine accumulation, passivation, and acetyl-mask survival must be measured; a generic iron label alone does not establish viability."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Conditional discharge source of cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K bulk collection environment; transient discharge plasma is much hotter", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Anoxic methane/ammonia-containing gas exposed to electrical discharge, followed by prompt collection into water. This is not a 300-330 K thermal reaction."}, "mechanism": "A coarse-grained radical and ion chemistry channel incorporates methane carbon and ammonia nitrogen into cyanamide while eliminating hydrogen.", "reasoning": "Primitive-Earth simulation production of cyanamide has historical precedent (Schimpl, Lemmon and Calvin, 1965), but that does not validate this exact simplified feed composition or net channel. This node is explicitly speculative and is not an elementary reaction or a claimed clean conversion. If the temperature constraint excludes transient plasma chemistry, pathways using this node are outside that stricter interpretation."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Cyanogen ammonolysis to cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich water, proposed pH 8-9, low cyanogen inventory, and rapid capture after gas dissolution."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia attacks cyanogen, with cyanide displacement giving cyanamide and HCN after proton transfer.", "reasoning": "Cyanogen cleavage by ammonia provides a chemically distinct cyanamide source without a hydrogenation catalyst. Its environmental implementation remains conditional: cyanogen supply is stipulated, hydrolysis competes, and actual cyanamide accumulation must be demonstrated. This route should not be interpreted as evidence that cyanogen was abundant in surface pools."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Methanol capture of cyanamide as O-methylisourea", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_023"], "outputs": ["mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen chloride acting as a protonating acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydrogen chloride", "aqueous": false, "other": "Methanol-rich, low-water acidic pore liquid; product is initially present as an isourea salt. Volatile retention and subsequent pH adjustment are required."}, "mechanism": "Pinner-type alcohol addition to protonated cyanamide produces an O-methylisourea framework.", "reasoning": "This is a recognizable laboratory activation chemistry, but its prebiotic implementation is weakly supported. Low water activity, substantial methanol, and strong acid must coexist without rapid volatilization or hydrolysis. It is included as a conditional alternative, not as an established evaporitic-pool process."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Ethanol capture of cyanamide as O-ethylisourea", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_024"], "outputs": ["mol_027"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen chloride acting as a protonating acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydrogen chloride", "aqueous": false, "other": "Ethanol-rich, low-water acidic liquid; isourea salt formation precedes transfer into an alkaline aqueous reaction compartment."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted ethanol addition to the cyanamide nitrile carbon forms O-ethylisourea.", "reasoning": "The chemical transformation follows the same established activation family as the methyl analogue, but ethanol availability and low-water acid chemistry are independent environmental constraints. This branch is speculative geochemically and offers no demonstrated advantage unless its feedstock and persistence are locally superior."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Methanethiol addition to cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_028"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium as an electrophile-activating acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm with replenished methanethiol vapor", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-3, concentrated cyanamide, and continuous thiol delivery from a nearby sulfur-bearing gas source. Evaporation alone cannot retain methanethiol."}, "mechanism": "Thiol attack on protonated cyanamide generates an S-methylisothiourea framework after proton transfer.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically plausible sulfur analogue of alcohol capture, but accumulation in the stated aqueous window is speculative. Methanethiol volatility, cyanamide hydrolysis, and actual addition kinetics are unresolved. The sulfur reagent is explicit rather than attributed to an unspecified mineral catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Ethanethiol addition to cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_025"], "outputs": ["mol_029"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium as an electrophile-activating acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-308 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-3, sustained ethanethiol supply, and sheltered liquid interfaces limiting gas loss."}, "mechanism": "Ethanethiol adds to activated cyanamide to produce S-ethylisothiourea.", "reasoning": "Speculative environmental implementation of a plausible thiol-addition route. Ethanethiol is less volatile than methanethiol but still requires a demonstrated source and retention mechanism. No prebiotic yield or dominance over cyanamide hydrolysis is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Direct cyanamide guanidination of alpha-protected ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_030"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "315-350 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 9.5-10.5, concentrated reactants, and days-to-weeks residence time. Keep the substrate hydrated to limit lactamization."}, "mechanism": "The terminal primary amine adds to cyanamide, followed by proton transfers that generate the guanidino group.", "reasoning": "Amine guanidination by cyanamide is chemically established, but this exact protected substrate and environmental window require verification. Crucially, the alpha nitrogen is an amide before the terminal amine is unveiled; side-chain selectivity is therefore chemically motivated rather than assumed for an unprotected diamine. Cyanamide dimerization, hydrolysis, and slow reaction remain competitors."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "O-methylisourea-mediated guanidination", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_026"], "outputs": ["mol_030", "mol_023"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 10-11 following neutralization of the isourea-forming compartment; hours-to-days reaction time."}, "mechanism": "The unmasked terminal amine attacks the isourea carbon, displacing methanol after proton transfer.", "reasoning": "O-alkylisoureas are established guanyl-transfer reagents. Alpha acetylation removes the principal two-amine regioselectivity problem, but neither reagent production nor reaction yield under these natural conditions is established. Hydrolysis of the donor can consume it before productive capture."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "O-ethylisourea-mediated guanidination", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_027"], "outputs": ["mol_030", "mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 10-11, concentrated protected ornithine, and hours-to-days residence time."}, "mechanism": "Terminal-amine substitution at the isourea carbon gives the protected guanidino amino acid and ethanol.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically distinct guanyl donor within a known reaction family. It does not require selective reaction of two free amines. Its environmental merit depends on actual O-ethylisourea availability and lifetime; these have not been demonstrated for the proposed setting."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "S-methylisothiourea-mediated guanidination", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_028"], "outputs": ["mol_030", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, hours-to-days residence time, and gas exchange permitting released methanethiol to escape."}, "mechanism": "The terminal amine replaces the methylthio group at the electrophilic guanyl carbon, releasing methanethiol.", "reasoning": "S-alkylisothiourea guanyl transfer is conventional synthetic chemistry and avoids an unspecified copper catalyst. Protection of the alpha amine supplies a real selectivity rationale. The weak point is environmental production and persistence of the donor, not a demonstrated prebiotic reaction sequence."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "S-ethylisothiourea-mediated guanidination", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_029"], "outputs": ["mol_030", "mol_025"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, concentrated protected ornithine, and hours-to-days reaction time."}, "mechanism": "Nucleophilic guanyl transfer from S-ethylisothiourea to the terminal amine releases ethanethiol.", "reasoning": "The bond-forming chemistry is supported at the reaction-family level, while this substrate-specific environmental implementation remains speculative. No claim is made that the ethyl donor is more prebiotically abundant or more effective than the methyl donor."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Acidic alpha-deacetylation to release arginine", "inputs": ["mol_030", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_032", "mol_031"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium from concentrated acidic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "355-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed 1-6 mol/L strong acid and days-to-weeks or longer residence time. Sustained acid concentration requires a specific volcanic-condensate setting; final neutralization changes protonation state but not molecular connectivity."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of the alpha N-acetyl amide releases acetate/acetic acid and the alpha amino group.", "reasoning": "Ordinary amide hydrolysis provides a stronger chemical basis than assuming mild spontaneous hydantoin or carbamoyl deprotection. Acidic rather than strongly alkaline treatment is chosen to reduce the risk of guanidino-group destruction. Nevertheless, adequate deacetylation at or below 370 K while retaining arginine must be demonstrated experimentally. This remains a shared endpoint bottleneck; authentic arginine standards and structural analysis, not formula matching alone, are required."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Discharge-derived cyanamide with direct guanidination", "description": "Prepare the protected ornithine skeleton, obtain cyanamide through the conditional discharge channel, guanidinate directly, and hydrolyze the acetyl mask. This minimizes donor activation steps but inherits the uncertain discharge source.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_014", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanogen-derived cyanamide with direct guanidination", "description": "Use cyanogen ammonolysis to supply cyanamide for direct reaction with alpha-protected ornithine. This is the least elaborated non-discharge candidate, conditional on cyanogen availability and validation of the shared skeleton and deprotection steps.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_014", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Discharge cyanamide through O-methylisourea", "description": "Convert discharge-derived cyanamide into O-methylisourea before guanyl transfer to the protected amino acid. Low-water methanolic acid chemistry is an additional environmental bottleneck.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_015", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_026", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Cyanogen cyanamide through O-methylisourea", "description": "Combine cyanogen ammonolysis with methanol-based guanyl-donor activation, protected side-chain guanidination, and final deacetylation. No step is claimed to establish the entire environmental sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_015", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_026", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Discharge cyanamide through O-ethylisourea", "description": "Use ethanol capture of discharge-derived cyanamide to form an alternative guanyl donor. The route requires separate acidic low-water and alkaline aqueous compartments.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011", "rxn_016", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_027", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Cyanogen cyanamide through O-ethylisourea", "description": "Generate cyanamide by cyanogen ammonolysis and activate it with ethanol before protected guanidination. Its distinctness concerns donor chemistry and precursor provenance, not an independent solution to the common bottlenecks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_016", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_027", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Discharge cyanamide through S-methylisothiourea", "description": "Capture discharge-derived cyanamide with methanethiol, then transfer the guanyl group to the sole unmasked amine. Thiol availability and donor accumulation require experimental confirmation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_012", "rxn_017", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_028", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Cyanogen cyanamide through S-methylisothiourea", "description": "Combine cyanogen ammonolysis, methanethiol-based donor formation, protected guanidination, and acid deacetylation. This avoids plasma chemistry but retains an unverified aqueous thiol-addition step.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_017", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_018", "mol_022", "mol_028", "mol_030"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Discharge cyanamide through S-ethylisothiourea", "description": "Use ethanethiol to prepare a sulfur-based guanyl donor from discharge-derived cyanamide. This is a conditional alternative for a setting with an independently justified ethanethiol supply.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_013", "rxn_018", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediat