e speculative reductions or competing cyclic chemistry before ornithinonitrile formation.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.57\n- Feedback: Not approved: overall confidence is below 0.90. Structural validity is strong: the 32 molecule nodes and 19 reaction nodes form a connected network, and the stated branches provide chemically interpretable routes to the target. The deterministic check establishes zero conservation violations. Record its inferred coefficients explicitly for rxn_001, rxn_007 and rxn_008; those coefficients are modest and not chemically suspicious. Resolve mol_017's formula/InChI discrepancy and repair mol_031's unparseable InChI, without treating either representation defect as an additional balance failure. The proposed carbon accounting is correct, the Strecker step can generate the required racemic center, and alpha-acetylation before nitro reduction logically addresses competing amino-group guanidination. The main limitations are implementation and evidence: rxn_002 requires substrate-specific control of Michael addition versus carbonyl addition, repeated addition and polymerization; rxn_005 requires complete hydrolysis compatible with the remaining functionality; rxn_006 requires productive thioester aminolysis; and rxn_007 requires demonstrably active iron surfaces, sufficient acidity and preservation of the acetyl group. Acidic deacetylation in rxn_019 is chemically reasonable, not intrinsically invalid, but its rate and yield within the proposed environment are unestablished. No numerical environmental window is actually supplied. Surface and Hydrothermal labels plus anoxia do not specify temperature, pH, pressure, concentrations, duration or catalyst phases. Separate compartments are acceptable, but hydrothermal thioester transfer, dilution/hydrolysis losses, pH adjustment and management of residual ammonia, metals and acylating reagent need physical justification. Discharge cyanamide production and O-isourea preparation are additional weakly supported branches. P8 and P10 are effectively tied leading candidates, not demonstrated complete syntheses. Listed problematic reactions indicate chemical or implementation uncertainties, not conservation violations. Feedstocks are accepted as stipulated; deductions concern reaction performance and compatibility rather than feedstock identity, hub counts or environment ordering. No research findings or citations were supplied for a separate literature-alignment assessment. Overall confidence reflects unresolved coupled bottlenecks across the complete network, not the number of alternative graph pathways.\n- Issues: ['The protected-ornithine strategy gives the correct carbon skeleton and avoids competing guanidination of a free alpha-amino group. However, rxn_008 is only a balanced discharge-source hypothesis, not an established preparation at useful cyanamide concentration. Direct guanidination is plausible but condition-sensitive. Shared uncertainties in nitro-Michael selectivity, backbone processing and final deacetylation prevent confidence in complete arginine production.', 'Cyanogen ammonolysis is a chemically credible cyanamide source and is better mechanistically specified than the discharge alternative. The route remains conditional on efficient direct guanidination and the common backbone chemistry. Excess ammonia can compete for guanylating reagent, while cyanamide hydrolysis or oligomerization can reduce productive conversion. Final deacetylation has the correct chemistry but lacks an explicit operating window.', 'O-methylisourea is a credible guanyl donor once prepared. Its formation cannot, however, be inferred simply from combining cyanamide and methanol: acid activation, solvent composition and water activity are important. Neither those conditions nor donor survival during subsequent pH adjustment are specified. The uncertain discharge source and shared backbone/deacetylation requirements remain additional limitations.', 'The cyanogen source removes the discharge-specific uncertainty, and guanidination with an available O-methylisourea donor is chemically credible. The principal branch-specific gap is preparing that donor under justified acidic, methanol-containing conditions. A workable transition from ammonia-containing source chemistry to donor preparation and then amine-compatible guanidination has not been demonstrated.', 'An available O-ethylisourea donor could plausibly guanidinate the exposed terminal amine. Its preparation from cyanamide and ethanol still requires specified activation conditions and control of water-dependent competing reactions. No supplied evidence supports treating this alkyl variant as more or less viable than P3. Both retain the uncertain discharge source and common route bottlenecks.', 'Cyanogen ammonolysis and subsequent guanidination by a prepared O-ethylisourea donor are plausible transformations. The unresolved link is donor preparation under environmentally justified acid/ethanol conditions, followed by handling that preserves the donor and avoids competition from excess ammonia. The ethyl substitution does not itself establish an advantage over P4.', 'Acid-assisted methanethiol addition to cyanamide is a plausible route to an isothiouronium donor, and the resulting guanyl-transfer chemistry is credible. Nevertheless, acidity, thiol retention, donor speciation and subsequent pH adjustment are unspecified. The discharge source remains particularly uncertain, and activated guanidination does not resolve the shared carbon-skeleton or deacetylation bottlenecks.', 'This is a leading conditional branch, effectively tied with P10. Cyanogen ammonolysis provides a chemically credible cyanamide source, while a prepared isothiouronium donor offers credible terminal-amine guanidination. Donor preparation and handling still require validation. The common nitro-Michael, hydrolysis, acetylation, iron-reduction and final deacetylation requirements prevent approval of the complete route.', 'The S-ethylisothiourea branch has the same credible guanyl-transfer rationale as P7. There is insufficient supplied evidence to distinguish their practical performance. Acid-assisted donor preparation and donor survival must be established, while the discharge cyanamide source remains weakly specified. All common backbone and final-deprotection uncertainties persist.', 'This branch is effectively tied with P8: the cyanogen source and isothiouronium-mediated guanidination are chemically credible, but neither establishes end-to-end production. No evidence supplied here warrants preferring the methyl or ethyl donor intrinsically. Explicit donor-preparation conditions and validation of the shared backbone and final deacetylation remain necessary.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct a racemic ornithine skeleton by conjugate hydrocyanation of acrolein, capture the aldehyde as a hydantoin, and only then reduce the remaining side-chain nitrile. This ordering avoids selective partial nitrile reduction, free 4-aminobutanal, and guanidination of an unprotected alpha-amino group. Five guanyl donors are evaluated at two genuinely different masking stages: the intact hydantoin and its ring-opened N-alpha-carbamoyl derivative. These combinations give ten distinct conditional pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses; methyl/ethyl donor pairs are homologous alternatives. No supplied evidence establishes an end-to-end prebiotic arginine synthesis. Numerical conditions below are proposed experimental screening windows, not reported substrate-specific yields or proven geological operating windows. The principal unresolved requirements are selective acrolein hydrocyanation, activity of a natural nickel-bearing phase toward the protected nitrile, donor av