m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct a racemic ornithine skeleton by conjugate hydrocyanation of acrolein, capture the aldehyde as a hydantoin, and only then reduce the remaining side-chain nitrile. This ordering avoids selective partial nitrile reduction, free 4-aminobutanal, and guanidination of an unprotected alpha-amino group. Five guanyl donors are evaluated at two genuinely different masking stages: the intact hydantoin and its ring-opened N-alpha-carbamoyl derivative. These combinations give ten distinct conditional pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses; methyl/ethyl donor pairs are homologous alternatives. No supplied evidence establishes an end-to-end prebiotic arginine synthesis. Numerical conditions below are proposed experimental screening windows, not reported substrate-specific yields or proven geological operating windows. The principal unresolved requirements are selective acrolein hydrocyanation, activity of a natural nickel-bearing phase toward the protected nitrile, donor availability, guanidination selectivity, and productive nonenzymatic unmasking. Simple feedstocks are stipulated rather than assigned unsupported origins. All reactions are anoxic. Surface organics would require transport through